  <static seed="1927" source="Contrib/PCC/BasicMath/IntegerAndSquareRoot/identifySquareNumbers30.pg">
    <statement><p>Evaluate the following.</p>
<p><ol label="a.">
<li>
<p><m>\displaystyle{ \sqrt{{{\frac{25}{4}}}} }</m> = <fillin characters="13" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>\displaystyle{ \sqrt{{-{\frac{9}{16}}}} }</m> = <fillin characters="13" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>{\frac{5}{2}}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\text{not a real number}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p><lq/>Square root<rq/> is the opposite operation of <lq/>square.<rq/></p>
<p>Since <m>\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^{2} = \left(\frac{2}{3}\right)\left(\frac{2}{3}\right) = \frac{4}{9}</m>, we have: <m>\sqrt{\frac{4}{9}} = \frac{2}{3}</m>.</p>
<p>The answers are:</p>
<p><ol label="a.">
<li>
<p><m>\displaystyle{\sqrt{{{\frac{25}{4}}}} = {{\frac{5}{2}}} }</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>\displaystyle{\sqrt{{-{\frac{9}{16}}}} \text{ is not a real number} }</m></p>
</li>
</ol></p>
<p>Note that square root of a negative number does not exist. This is because any number squared is always positive or <m>0</m>.</p>
</solution>
